latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Preprocess the Input Grid:\n   a. Convert the 8x4 input grid into a 4x4 grid of 2x2 blocks.\n   b. For each 2x2 block, count the number of magenta (6) and gray (5) squares.\n\n2. Create a Heat Map:\n   a. Assign weights to each 2x2 block based on the count of magenta and gray squares.\n   b. Give higher weights to blocks near edges and corners.\n\n3. Determine the Number of Yellow Squares:\n   a. Calculate the total heat from the heat map.\n   b. If the total heat is above a certain threshold, use 4 yellow squares; otherwise, use 3.\n\n4. Identify Potential Yellow Square Positions:\n   a. Calculate an influence score for each position in the 4x4 output grid.\n   b. The influence score should consider the heat of the corresponding 2x2 block and its neighbors.\n\n5. Detect Strong Patterns:\n   a. Check for strong horizontal or vertical patterns in the input grid.\n   b. If a strong pattern is detected, consider aligning yellow squares accordingly.\n\n6. Place Yellow Squares:\n   a. Start with the position with the highest influence score.\n   b. Place a yellow square and mark adjacent positions as unavailable.\n   c. Repeat until all yellow squares are placed or no valid positions remain.\n\n7. Apply Special Rules:\n   a. If 3 yellow squares are placed, check if they form an L-shape or diagonal.\n   b. If not, consider adjusting their positions to form such patterns.\n\n8. Balance the Distribution:\n   a. Ensure yellow squares are well-distributed across the grid.\n   b. If all yellow squares are in one half of the grid, consider moving one to balance the distribution.\n\n9. Handle Edge Cases:\n   a. If no clear pattern emerges, fall back to placing yellow squares in corners or along edges.\n\n10. Generate the Output Grid:\n    a. Create a 4x4 grid with the placed yellow squares.\n    b. Fill the remaining positions with black (0).\n\n11. Validate the Solution:\n    a. Ensure no yellow squares are adjacent.\n    b. Check that the number of yellow squares matches the determined count.\n    c. Verify that the solution generally reflects the input pattern's distribution of magenta and gray squares.\n\n12. Refine and Iterate:\n    a. Test the solution against all provided test cases.\n    b. If any test case fails, analyze the discrepancy and adjust the algorithm accordingly.\n    c. Repeat this process until all test cases pass.\n\nThis plan attempts to capture the nuanced relationship between the input and output grids while allowing for the flexibility needed to handle various patterns. The key is to balance rule-based decisions with more abstract pattern recognition, which may require fine-tuning based on the results of each iteration."
